content:
A function $F$ is called an \textbf{antiderivative} of $f$ on an interval $I$ if:
\[
\boxed{F'(x) = f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in I}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

An antiderivative of $f$ is any function whose derivative equals $f$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Key Examples:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $f(x) = 2x$, then $F(x) = x^2$ is an antiderivative because $F'(x) = 2x$
    \item If $f(x) = \cos x$, then $F(x) = \sin x$ is an antiderivative because $F'(x) = \cos x$
    \item If $f(x) = e^x$, then $F(x) = e^x$ is an antiderivative because $F'(x) = e^x$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important Fact:}}

Antiderivatives are \textbf{not unique}! If $F$ is an antiderivative of $f$, then so is $F + C$ for any constant $C$, because:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}[F(x) + C] = F'(x) + 0 = f(x)
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
    % Show multiple antiderivatives (vertical shifts)
    \draw[->] (-0.5, 0) -- (4, 0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 3.5) node[above] {$y$};

    % Draw parabolas (antiderivatives of 2x)
    \draw[primaryblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3, samples=50] (\x, {0.3*\x*\x});
    \draw[primaryblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3, samples=50] (\x, {0.3*\x*\x + 0.5});
    \draw[primaryblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3, samples=50] (\x, {0.3*\x*\x + 1});
    \draw[primaryblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3, samples=50] (\x, {0.3*\x*\x + 1.5});

    % Labels
    \node[primaryblue] at (3.5, 2.7) {$F(x) = x^2 + C$};
    \node[primaryblue] at (3.5, 2.3) {$C = 3$};
    \node[primaryblue] at (3.5, 1.8) {$C = 2$};
    \node[primaryblue] at (3.5, 1.3) {$C = 1$};
    \node[primaryblue] at (3.5, 0.8) {$C = 0$};

    % Title
    \node[align=center] at (2, -1) {\textbf{Multiple Antiderivatives}\\All have derivative $f(x) = 2x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{indefinite integral} of $f$ with respect to $x$ is defined as:
\[
\boxed{\int f(x) \, dx = F(x) + C}
\]

where $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$ and $C$ is an arbitrary constant.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Notation and Terminology:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \node at (0,0) {$\displaystyle \int f(x) \, dx = F(x) + C$};

    % Labels
    \draw[->] (-1.5, -0.3) -- (-1.5, -0.8) node[below, align=center] {\small Integral};
    \draw[->] (-0.5, -0.3) -- (-0.5, -0.8) node[below, align=center] {\small Integrand};
    \draw[->] (0.3, -0.3) -- (0.3, -0.8) node[below, align=center] {\small Differential};
    \draw[->] (1.5, -0.3) -- (1.5, -0.8) node[below, align=center] {\small Antiderivative};
    \draw[->] (2.3, -0.3) -- (2.3, -0.8) node[below, align=center] {\small Constant of\\integration};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Reading:} ``The integral of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$ equals $F(x)$ plus $C$''

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Relationship to Differentiation:}}

Integration and differentiation are \textbf{inverse operations}:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int f(x) \, dx\right] = f(x)
\]
\[
\int f'(x) \, dx = f(x) + C
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Always Include $+ C$!}}

The constant of integration is crucial for indefinite integrals. Forgetting it is one of the most common mistakes in integration.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the general antiderivative (indefinite integral) of each function:

(a) $f(x) = 3$

(b) $f(x) = x^2$

(c) $f(x) = \cos x$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $\displaystyle \int 3 \, dx$

We need a function whose derivative is 3. Since $\dfrac{d}{dx}(3x) = 3$:

\[
\int 3 \, dx = 3x + C
\]

\textbf{Verification:} $\dfrac{d}{dx}(3x + C) = 3$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\displaystyle \int x^2 \, dx$

We need a function whose derivative is $x^2$. Since $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{x^3}{3}\right) = x^2$:

\[
\int x^2 \, dx = \frac{x^3}{3} + C
\]

\textbf{Verification:} $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{x^3}{3} + C\right) = x^2$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $\displaystyle \int \cos x \, dx$

We need a function whose derivative is $\cos x$. Since $\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sin x) = \cos x$:

\[
\int \cos x \, dx = \sin x + C
\]

\textbf{Verification:} $\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sin x + C) = \cos x$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{align*}
(a) \quad & \boxed{\int 3 \, dx = 3x + C} \\
(b) \quad & \boxed{\int x^2 \, dx = \frac{x^3}{3} + C} \\
(c) \quad & \boxed{\int \cos x \, dx = \sin x + C}
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the function $f$ such that $f'(x) = 2x + 1$ and $f(0) = 3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find the general antiderivative

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= \int (2x + 1) \, dx \\
&= \int 2x \, dx + \int 1 \, dx \\
&= x^2 + x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Use the initial condition to find $C$

Given $f(0) = 3$:
\begin{align*}
f(0) &= 0^2 + 0 + C = 3 \\
C &= 3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Write the specific solution

\[
f(x) = x^2 + x + 3
\]

\textbf{Verification:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f'(x) = 2x + 1$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item $f(0) = 0 + 0 + 3 = 3$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f(x) = x^2 + x + 3}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Idea:}} Initial conditions allow us to determine the specific value of $C$, giving us a \textbf{particular solution} rather than the general solution.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Multiple Notations:}

All of the following mean the same thing:
\begin{align*}
\int f(x) \, dx &= F(x) + C \\
\int f &= F + C \\
\text{Antiderivative of } f &= F + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Common Terminology:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Integrate $f$'' means find $\int f(x) \, dx$
    \item ``Find the antiderivative'' means find $\int f(x) \, dx$
    \item ``Evaluate the integral'' means compute $\int f(x) \, dx$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Warning About Notation:}}

The differential $dx$ is \textbf{essential}! It tells us:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Which variable we're integrating with respect to
    \item Where the integrand ends
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Examples:}
\begin{align*}
\int x^2 \, dx &\neq \int x^2 \, dt \\
\int 2x \, dx &= x^2 + C \\
\int 2 \, x \, dx &= x^2 + C \quad \text{(same as above)}
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.1 Summary}}

\textbf{Key Definitions:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Antiderivative:} $F'(x) = f(x)$
    \item \textbf{Indefinite Integral:} $\int f(x) \, dx = F(x) + C$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Fundamental Relationship:}
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int f(x) \, dx\right] = f(x)}
\]
\[
\boxed{\int f'(x) \, dx = f(x) + C}
\]

\textbf{Key Facts:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Integration is the reverse of differentiation
    \item Antiderivatives differ by a constant
    \item Always include $+ C$ for indefinite integrals
    \item Initial conditions determine specific value of $C$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting to add $+ C$
    \item Forgetting the differential $dx$
    \item Confusing $\int f(x) \, dx$ with $\int f(x) \, dt$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Check Your Understanding:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Can you explain why antiderivatives are not unique?
    \item What is the difference between $F(x)$ and $F(x) + C$?
    \item How do you verify an antiderivative is correct?
\end{itemize}

\newpage

\textbf{Power Rule for Integration:}
\[
\boxed{\int x^n \, dx = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C \quad (n \neq -1)}
\]

\textbf{Special Cases:}
\begin{align*}
\int k \, dx &= kx + C \quad \text{(constant)} \\
\int \frac{1}{x} \, dx &= \ln|x| + C \quad \text{(} n = -1 \text{ case)} \\
\int e^x \, dx &= e^x + C \\
\int a^x \, dx &= \frac{a^x}{\ln a} + C \quad (a > 0, a \neq 1)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Trigonometric Integrals:}
\begin{align*}
\int \sin x \, dx &= -\cos x + C \\
\int \cos x \, dx &= \sin x + C \\
\int \sec^2 x \, dx &= \tan x + C \\
\int \csc^2 x \, dx &= -\cot x + C \\
\int \sec x \tan x \, dx &= \sec x + C \\
\int \csc x \cot x \, dx &= -\csc x + C
\end{align*}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{How to Remember:}}

These formulas come directly from differentiation! For each derivative formula, there's a corresponding integral formula.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Watch the Signs:}}

Notice the negative signs in $\int \sin x \, dx = -\cos x + C$ and others. These come from the chain rule in reverse!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Constant Multiple Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\int cf(x) \, dx = c\int f(x) \, dx}
\]

Constants can be pulled out of integrals.

\textbf{Sum Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\int [f(x) + g(x)] \, dx = \int f(x) \, dx + \int g(x) \, dx}
\]

The integral of a sum is the sum of the integrals.

\textbf{Difference Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\int [f(x) - g(x)] \, dx = \int f(x) \, dx - \int g(x) \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Combined:}}

For constants $a$ and $b$:
\[
\int [af(x) + bg(x)] \, dx = a\int f(x) \, dx + b\int g(x) \, dx
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Warning:}}

There is \textbf{NO product rule or quotient rule for integration}! 
\[
\int f(x)g(x) \, dx \neq \left(\int f(x) \, dx\right)\left(\int g(x) \, dx\right)
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate each integral:

(a) $\displaystyle \int x^5 \, dx$

(b) $\displaystyle \int \frac{1}{x^3} \, dx$

(c) $\displaystyle \int \sqrt{x} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $\displaystyle \int x^5 \, dx$

Using the power rule with $n = 5$:
\[
\int x^5 \, dx = \frac{x^{5+1}}{5+1} + C = \frac{x^6}{6} + C
\]

\textbf{Verification:} $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{x^6}{6} + C\right) = x^5$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\displaystyle \int \frac{1}{x^3} \, dx = \int x^{-3} \, dx$

Using the power rule with $n = -3$:
\begin{align*}
\int x^{-3} \, dx &= \frac{x^{-3+1}}{-3+1} + C \\
&= \frac{x^{-2}}{-2} + C \\
&= -\frac{1}{2x^2} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Verification:} $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(-\dfrac{1}{2x^2} + C\right) = -\dfrac{1}{2} \cdot (-2)x^{-3} = x^{-3} = \dfrac{1}{x^3}$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $\displaystyle \int \sqrt{x} \, dx = \int x^{1/2} \, dx$

Using the power rule with $n = \dfrac{1}{2}$:
\begin{align*}
\int x^{1/2} \, dx &= \frac{x^{1/2+1}}{1/2+1} + C \\
&= \frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2} + C \\
&= \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2} + C \\
&= \frac{2}{3}x\sqrt{x} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{align*}
(a) \quad & \boxed{\frac{x^6}{6} + C} \\
(b) \quad & \boxed{-\frac{1}{2x^2} + C} \\
(c) \quad & \boxed{\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2} + C}
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate: $\displaystyle \int (3x^2 - 4x + 5) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Split using sum and difference rules

\begin{align*}
\int (3x^2 - 4x + 5) \, dx &= \int 3x^2 \, dx - \int 4x \, dx + \int 5 \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Pull out constants

\begin{align*}
&= 3\int x^2 \, dx - 4\int x \, dx + 5\int 1 \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Apply power rule to each term

\begin{align*}
&= 3 \cdot \frac{x^3}{3} - 4 \cdot \frac{x^2}{2} + 5x + C \\
&= x^3 - 2x^2 + 5x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Verification:}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(x^3 - 2x^2 + 5x + C) = 3x^2 - 4x + 5 \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{x^3 - 2x^2 + 5x + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} Even though we had three integrals, we only need \textbf{one} constant $C$ at the end!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate each integral:

(a) $\displaystyle \int \frac{x^3 + 2x^2 - 5}{x^2} \, dx$

(b) $\displaystyle \int (2\sqrt{x} + \frac{3}{\sqrt{x}}) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $\displaystyle \int \frac{x^3 + 2x^2 - 5}{x^2} \, dx$

\textbf{Step 1:} Simplify by dividing each term

\begin{align*}
\int \frac{x^3 + 2x^2 - 5}{x^2} \, dx &= \int \left(\frac{x^3}{x^2} + \frac{2x^2}{x^2} - \frac{5}{x^2}\right) dx \\
&= \int \left(x + 2 - 5x^{-2}\right) dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Integrate term by term

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{x^2}{2} + 2x - 5 \cdot \frac{x^{-1}}{-1} + C \\
&= \frac{x^2}{2} + 2x + \frac{5}{x} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{\frac{x^2}{2} + 2x + \frac{5}{x} + C}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\displaystyle \int (2\sqrt{x} + \frac{3}{\sqrt{x}}) \, dx$

\textbf{Step 1:} Rewrite using exponents

\begin{align*}
\int (2\sqrt{x} + \frac{3}{\sqrt{x}}) \, dx &= \int (2x^{1/2} + 3x^{-1/2}) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Integrate using power rule

\begin{align*}
&= 2 \cdot \frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2} + 3 \cdot \frac{x^{1/2}}{1/2} + C \\
&= \frac{4}{3}x^{3/2} + 6x^{1/2} + C \\
&= \frac{4}{3}x\sqrt{x} + 6\sqrt{x} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{\frac{4}{3}x^{3/2} + 6x^{1/2} + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Strategy:}} When integrals look complicated, try:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Simplifying algebraically first
    \item Rewriting roots as fractional exponents
    \item Splitting fractions into separate terms
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate:

(a) $\displaystyle \int (3\sin x - 2\cos x) \, dx$

(b) $\displaystyle \int (4\sec^2 x + \sec x \tan x) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $\displaystyle \int (3\sin x - 2\cos x) \, dx$

\begin{align*}
\int (3\sin x - 2\cos x) \, dx &= 3\int \sin x \, dx - 2\int \cos x \, dx \\
&= 3(-\cos x) - 2(\sin x) + C \\
&= -3\cos x - 2\sin x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{-3\cos x - 2\sin x + C}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\displaystyle \int (4\sec^2 x + \sec x \tan x) \, dx$

\begin{align*}
\int (4\sec^2 x + \sec x \tan x) \, dx &= 4\int \sec^2 x \, dx + \int \sec x \tan x \, dx \\
&= 4\tan x + \sec x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{4\tan x + \sec x + C}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification for (a):}}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(-3\cos x - 2\sin x + C) = 3\sin x - 2\cos x \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate:

(a) $\displaystyle \int (e^x + 2^x) \, dx$

(b) $\displaystyle \int \frac{3}{x} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $\displaystyle \int (e^x + 2^x) \, dx$

\begin{align*}
\int (e^x + 2^x) \, dx &= \int e^x \, dx + \int 2^x \, dx \\
&= e^x + \frac{2^x}{\ln 2} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{e^x + \frac{2^x}{\ln 2} + C}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\displaystyle \int \frac{3}{x} \, dx$

\begin{align*}
\int \frac{3}{x} \, dx &= 3\int \frac{1}{x} \, dx \\
&= 3\ln|x| + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{3\ln|x| + C}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}} For $\int \frac{1}{x} \, dx$, always use $\ln|x|$ (with absolute value) to handle both positive and negative $x$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.2 Summary: Basic Integration Rules}}

\textbf{Essential Formulas:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|p{5cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Function} & \textbf{Integral} \\
\hline
$x^n$ $(n \neq -1)$ & $\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$ \\
\hline
$\dfrac{1}{x}$ & $\ln|x| + C$ \\
\hline
$e^x$ & $e^x + C$ \\
\hline
$a^x$ & $\dfrac{a^x}{\ln a} + C$ \\
\hline
$\sin x$ & $-\cos x + C$ \\
\hline
$\cos x$ & $\sin x + C$ \\
\hline
$\sec^2 x$ & $\tan x + C$ \\
\hline
$\sec x \tan x$ & $\sec x + C$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Linearity Rules:}
\begin{align*}
\int cf(x) \, dx &= c\int f(x) \, dx \\
\int [f(x) \pm g(x)] \, dx &= \int f(x) \, dx \pm \int g(x) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Integration Strategy:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Simplify the integrand algebraically
    \item Rewrite roots as fractional exponents
    \item Split into sum of simpler terms
    \item Apply appropriate integration formulas
    \item Don't forget $+ C$!
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting $+ C$
    \item Using $\ln x$ instead of $\ln|x|$ for $\int \frac{1}{x} dx$
    \item Trying to apply product/quotient rules (they don't exist!)
    \item Wrong sign in $\int \sin x \, dx = -\cos x + C$ (not $+\cos x$)
    \item Forgetting to simplify before integrating
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Always Verify:}} Differentiate your answer to check if you get back the original integrand!

\newpage

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $u$ equal the ``inside function'' (usually the more complicated part)
    \item Look for a function whose derivative appears elsewhere in the integrand
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Differentiate: $du = g'(x) \, dx$
    \item If necessary, solve for $dx$: $dx = \frac{du}{g'(x)}$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Replace all $x$ terms with $u$ terms
    \item Replace $dx$ with $du$ (or appropriate multiple)
    \item Check: the integral should now be entirely in terms of $u$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Integrate with respect to $u$
    \item Don't forget $+ C$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Replace $u$ with the original expression in $x$
    \item Final answer should be in terms of $x$ only
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{When to Use Substitution:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Composition of functions (one inside another)
    \item You see a function and its derivative (or a constant multiple)
    \item Complex expressions that would simplify with substitution
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int 2x(x^2 + 1)^5 \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}

Let $u = x^2 + 1$ (the inside function)

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\
du &= 2x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

Notice that $2x \, dx$ appears in the original integral!

\begin{align*}
\int 2x(x^2 + 1)^5 \, dx &= \int (x^2 + 1)^5 \cdot 2x \, dx \\
&= \int u^5 \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\int u^5 \, du &= \frac{u^6}{6} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{(x^2 + 1)^6}{6} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{(x^2 + 1)^6}{6} + C}

\textbf{Verification:}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{(x^2+1)^6}{6}\right] = \frac{6(x^2+1)^5 \cdot 2x}{6} = 2x(x^2+1)^5 \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int x(x^2 + 3)^4 \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}

Let $u = x^2 + 3$

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
du &= 2x \, dx \\
x \, dx &= \frac{1}{2} du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

\begin{align*}
\int x(x^2 + 3)^4 \, dx &= \int (x^2 + 3)^4 \cdot x \, dx \\
&= \int u^4 \cdot \frac{1}{2} du \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\int u^4 \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\int u^4 \, du &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{u^5}{5} + C \\
&= \frac{u^5}{10} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{(x^2 + 3)^5}{10} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{(x^2 + 3)^5}{10} + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Point:}} Even though we had $x \, dx$ but needed $2x \, dx$, we adjusted by multiplying by $\frac{1}{2}$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \sin^3 x \cos x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}

Let $u = \sin x$ (note: $\cos x$ is the derivative of $\sin x$)

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
du &= \cos x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

\begin{align*}
\int \sin^3 x \cos x \, dx &= \int (\sin x)^3 \cdot \cos x \, dx \\
&= \int u^3 \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\int u^3 \, du &= \frac{u^4}{4} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{\sin^4 x}{4} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{\sin^4 x}{4} + C}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{\sin^4 x}{4}\right] = \frac{4\sin^3 x \cos x}{4} = \sin^3 x \cos x \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int xe^{x^2} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}

Let $u = x^2$ (the exponent)

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
du &= 2x \, dx \\
x \, dx &= \frac{1}{2} du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

\begin{align*}
\int xe^{x^2} \, dx &= \int e^{x^2} \cdot x \, dx \\
&= \int e^u \cdot \frac{1}{2} du \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\int e^u \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\int e^u \, du &= \frac{1}{2}e^u + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{1}{2}e^{x^2} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{1}{2}e^{x^2} + C}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \frac{2x + 3}{x^2 + 3x + 5} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Recognize the pattern}

Notice: derivative of denominator is $2x + 3$ (exactly the numerator!)

Let $u = x^2 + 3x + 5$

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
du &= (2x + 3) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

\begin{align*}
\int \frac{2x + 3}{x^2 + 3x + 5} \, dx &= \int \frac{1}{u} \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\int \frac{1}{u} \, du &= \ln|u| + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \ln|x^2 + 3x + 5| + C
\end{align*}

Since $x^2 + 3x + 5 = (x + \frac{3}{2})^2 + \frac{11}{4} > 0$ always, we can drop absolute value:

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\ln(x^2 + 3x + 5) + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Pattern:}} Whenever you see $\displaystyle \int \frac{f'(x)}{f(x)} \, dx$, the answer is $\ln|f(x)| + C$

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$}

Let $u = x^2 + 1$

\textbf{Step 2: Find $du$}

\begin{align*}
du &= 2x \, dx \\
x \, dx &= \frac{1}{2} du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Substitute}

\begin{align*}
\int \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}} \, dx &= \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}} \cdot x \, dx \\
&= \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{u}} \cdot \frac{1}{2} du \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\int u^{-1/2} \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Integrate}

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\int u^{-1/2} \, du &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{u^{1/2}}{1/2} + C \\
&= u^{1/2} + C \\
&= \sqrt{u} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Back-substitute}

\begin{align*}
&= \sqrt{x^2 + 1} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\sqrt{x^2 + 1} + C}

\textbf{Verification:}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}[\sqrt{x^2+1}] = \frac{2x}{2\sqrt{x^2+1}} = \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+1}} \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.3 Summary: Integration by Substitution}}

\textbf{Substitution Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\int f(g(x)) g'(x) \, dx = \int f(u) \, du \quad \text{where } u = g(x)}
\]

\textbf{5-Step Method:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Choose $u$ (usually the inside function)
    \item Find $du = g'(x) \, dx$
    \item Substitute (convert everything to $u$)
    \item Integrate with respect to $u$
    \item Back-substitute (replace $u$ with expression in $x$)
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common Patterns:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\int f(g(x)) g'(x) \, dx$ $\to$ let $u = g(x)$
    \item $\int \frac{f'(x)}{f(x)} \, dx = \ln|f(x)| + C$
    \item Look for function and its derivative
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Exam Alert:}} $u$-substitution appears in \textbf{almost every exam}! Master this technique.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Not adjusting coefficients (forgetting the $\frac{1}{2}$, etc.)
    \item Forgetting to back-substitute at the end
    \item Mixing $x$ and $u$ in the same integral
    \item Not completely converting to $u$ before integrating
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Practice Tip:}} Always verify by differentiating your answer!

\newpage

If $u$ and $v$ are differentiable functions of $x$, then:
\[
\boxed{\int u \, dv = uv - \int v \, du}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Derivation from Product Rule:}}

Starting with the product rule:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(uv) = u \frac{dv}{dx} + v \frac{du}{dx}
\]

Rearranging:
\[
u \frac{dv}{dx} = \frac{d}{dx}(uv) - v \frac{du}{dx}
\]

Integrating both sides:
\begin{align*}
\int u \frac{dv}{dx} \, dx &= \int \frac{d}{dx}(uv) \, dx - \int v \frac{du}{dx} \, dx \\
\int u \, dv &= uv - \int v \, du
\end{align*}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Alternative Notation:}}
\[
\int f(x)g(x) \, dx = f(x)G(x) - \int f'(x)G(x) \, dx
\]

where $G(x)$ is an antiderivative of $g(x)$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Step 1: Identify $u$ and $dv$}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Choose which part to call $u$ and which to call $dv$
    \item Use the LIATE rule (see below) as a guide
    \item Must have: $\int u \, dv = \int [\text{product}] \, dx$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Compute $du$ and $v$}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Differentiate: $du = u' \, dx$
    \item Integrate: $v = \int dv$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply the formula}
\[
\int u \, dv = uv - \int v \, du
\]

\textbf{Step 4: Evaluate $\int v \, du$}
\begin{itemize}
    \item This should be simpler than the original integral
    \item If not, try different choices for $u$ and $dv$
    \item Sometimes need to apply parts multiple times
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 5: Simplify and add $+ C$}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{LIATE Rule for Choosing $u$:}

When the integrand is a product, choose $u$ in this priority order:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
\textbf{L} & Logarithmic functions ($\ln x$, $\log x$) \\
\textbf{I} & Inverse trig functions ($\arcsin x$, $\arctan x$) \\
\textbf{A} & Algebraic functions ($x$, $x^2$, $\sqrt{x}$) \\
\textbf{T} & Trigonometric functions ($\sin x$, $\cos x$) \\
\textbf{E} & Exponential functions ($e^x$, $2^x$) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Why LIATE?}}

Functions higher in the list simplify when differentiated; those lower simplify (or stay the same) when integrated.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int x e^x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$ and $dv$}

Using LIATE: $x$ is algebraic (A), $e^x$ is exponential (E)

Choose: $u = x$ and $dv = e^x \, dx$

\textbf{Step 2: Compute $du$ and $v$}

\begin{align*}
du &= dx \\
v &= \int e^x \, dx = e^x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply formula}

\begin{align*}
\int x e^x \, dx &= uv - \int v \, du \\
&= x e^x - \int e^x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Evaluate remaining integral}

\begin{align*}
&= x e^x - e^x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5: Simplify}

\begin{align*}
&= e^x(x - 1) + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{e^x(x-1) + C}

\textbf{Verification:}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}[e^x(x-1)] = e^x(x-1) + e^x \cdot 1 = e^x \cdot x \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int x \sin x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Choose $u$ and $dv$}

Using LIATE: $x$ is algebraic (A), $\sin x$ is trig (T)

Choose: $u = x$ and $dv = \sin x \, dx$

\textbf{Step 2: Compute $du$ and $v$}

\begin{align*}
du &= dx \\
v &= \int \sin x \, dx = -\cos x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply formula}

\begin{align*}
\int x \sin x \, dx &= uv - \int v \, du \\
&= x(-\cos x) - \int (-\cos x) \, dx \\
&= -x\cos x + \int \cos x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Evaluate}

\begin{align*}
&= -x\cos x + \sin x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{-x\cos x + \sin x + C}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}[-x\cos x + \sin x] = -\cos x + x\sin x + \cos x = x\sin x \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \ln x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Rewrite and choose}

Rewrite as: $\int \ln x \cdot 1 \, dx$

Using LIATE: $\ln x$ is logarithmic (L), $1$ is algebraic (A)

Choose: $u = \ln x$ and $dv = 1 \, dx$

\textbf{Step 2: Compute $du$ and $v$}

\begin{align*}
du &= \frac{1}{x} \, dx \\
v &= \int 1 \, dx = x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply formula}

\begin{align*}
\int \ln x \, dx &= uv - \int v \, du \\
&= x \ln x - \int x \cdot \frac{1}{x} \, dx \\
&= x \ln x - \int 1 \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4: Evaluate}

\begin{align*}
&= x \ln x - x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{x\ln x - x + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Technique:}} For $\int \ln x \, dx$, treat $\ln x$ as the ``$u$'' and let $dv = dx$. This is a standard trick for logarithms!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int x^2 e^x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{First Application:}

Choose: $u = x^2$, $dv = e^x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
du &= 2x \, dx \\
v &= e^x
\end{align*}

Apply formula:
\begin{align*}
\int x^2 e^x \, dx &= x^2 e^x - \int e^x \cdot 2x \, dx \\
&= x^2 e^x - 2\int x e^x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Second Application:}

For $\int x e^x \, dx$, choose: $u = x$, $dv = e^x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
du &= dx \\
v &= e^x
\end{align*}

Apply formula:
\begin{align*}
\int x e^x \, dx &= xe^x - \int e^x \, dx \\
&= xe^x - e^x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Combine:}

\begin{align*}
\int x^2 e^x \, dx &= x^2 e^x - 2(xe^x - e^x) + C \\
&= x^2 e^x - 2xe^x + 2e^x + C \\
&= e^x(x^2 - 2x + 2) + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{e^x(x^2 - 2x + 2) + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Pattern:}} When integrating $x^n e^x$, you need to apply parts $n$ times!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int x^3 \sin x \, dx$ using tabular method.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Tabular Method:} (useful when one function eventually becomes zero upon differentiation)

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Sign} & \textbf{Differentiate ($u$)} & \textbf{Integrate ($dv$)} \\
\hline
$+$ & $x^3$ & $\sin x$ \\
\hline
$-$ & $3x^2$ & $-\cos x$ \\
\hline
$+$ & $6x$ & $-\sin x$ \\
\hline
$-$ & $6$ & $\cos x$ \\
\hline
$+$ & $0$ & $\sin x$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Multiply diagonally with alternating signs:}

\begin{align*}
\int x^3 \sin x \, dx &= (+)(x^3)(-\cos x) + (-)(3x^2)(-\sin x) \\
&\quad + (+)(6x)(\cos x) + (-)(6)(\sin x) + C \\
&= -x^3\cos x + 3x^2\sin x + 6x\cos x - 6\sin x + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{-x^3\cos x + 3x^2\sin x + 6x\cos x - 6\sin x + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{When to Use Tabular Method:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Polynomial times sine, cosine, or exponential
    \item One function becomes zero after repeated differentiation
    \item Saves time compared to repeated integration by parts
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int e^x \sin x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{First Application:}

Choose: $u = \sin x$, $dv = e^x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
du &= \cos x \, dx \\
v &= e^x
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
\int e^x \sin x \, dx &= e^x \sin x - \int e^x \cos x \, dx \quad (*)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Second Application:}

For $\int e^x \cos x \, dx$, choose: $u = \cos x$, $dv = e^x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
du &= -\sin x \, dx \\
v &= e^x
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
\int e^x \cos x \, dx &= e^x \cos x - \int e^x(-\sin x) \, dx \\
&= e^x \cos x + \int e^x \sin x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Substitute back into (*):}

\begin{align*}
\int e^x \sin x \, dx &= e^x \sin x - \left(e^x \cos x + \int e^x \sin x \, dx\right) \\
\int e^x \sin x \, dx &= e^x \sin x - e^x \cos x - \int e^x \sin x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Solve for the integral:}

\begin{align*}
2\int e^x \sin x \, dx &= e^x \sin x - e^x \cos x \\
\int e^x \sin x \, dx &= \frac{e^x(\sin x - \cos x)}{2} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{e^x(\sin x - \cos x)}{2} + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Cyclic Technique:}} When the integral reappears, bring it to one side and solve algebraically!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.4 Summary: Integration by Parts}}

\textbf{Integration by Parts Formula:}
\[
\boxed{\int u \, dv = uv - \int v \, du}
\]

\textbf{LIATE Rule (for choosing $u$):}
\begin{center}
\textbf{L}ogarithmic $>$ \textbf{I}nverse trig $>$ \textbf{A}lgebraic $>$ \textbf{T}rig $>$ \textbf{E}xponential
\end{center}

\textbf{When to Use Integration by Parts:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Product of two functions
    \item Substitution doesn't work
    \item Functions from different categories (poly $\times$ trig, poly $\times$ exp, etc.)
    \item Logarithmic or inverse trig functions (even alone!)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Special Cases:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\int \ln x \, dx = x\ln x - x + C$
    \item $\int x^n e^x \, dx$ requires $n$ applications
    \item $\int e^x \sin x \, dx$ or $\int e^x \cos x \, dx$ use cyclic technique
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Wrong choice of $u$ and $dv$ (violating LIATE)
    \item Forgetting to include $- \int v \, du$ term
    \item Sign errors (especially with trig functions)
    \item Not recognizing when integral is getting more complicated (try different $u$)
    \item In cyclic method: forgetting to bring integral to one side
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Pro Tips:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Always verify by differentiation
    \item Tabular method saves time for repeated parts
    \item If integral gets worse, try different $u$ and $dv$
    \item Watch for cyclic patterns with $e^x \sin x$ or $e^x \cos x$
\end{itemize}

\newpage

\textbf{Pythagorean Identities:}
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x &= 1 \\
1 + \tan^2 x &= \sec^2 x \\
1 + \cot^2 x &= \csc^2 x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Double Angle Formulas:}
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x &= \frac{1 - \cos(2x)}{2} \\
\cos^2 x &= \frac{1 + \cos(2x)}{2} \\
\sin x \cos x &= \frac{\sin(2x)}{2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Product-to-Sum Formulas:}
\begin{align*}
\sin A \sin B &= \frac{1}{2}[\cos(A-B) - \cos(A+B)] \\
\cos A \cos B &= \frac{1}{2}[\cos(A-B) + \cos(A+B)] \\
\sin A \cos B &= \frac{1}{2}[\sin(A+B) + \sin(A-B)]
\end{align*}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{When to Use:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Use Pythagorean identities to convert between $\sin$ and $\cos$
    \item Use double angle formulas to reduce powers
    \item Use product-to-sum to eliminate products
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Type 1: $\int \sin^m x \cos^n x \, dx$}

\textbf{Case 1a:} If $m$ is odd
\begin{itemize}
    \item Save one $\sin x$ for $du$
    \item Convert rest using $\sin^2 x = 1 - \cos^2 x$
    \item Substitute $u = \cos x$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 1b:} If $n$ is odd
\begin{itemize}
    \item Save one $\cos x$ for $du$
    \item Convert rest using $\cos^2 x = 1 - \sin^2 x$
    \item Substitute $u = \sin x$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 1c:} If both $m$ and $n$ are even
\begin{itemize}
    \item Use power-reducing formulas:
    \begin{align*}
    \sin^2 x &= \frac{1 - \cos(2x)}{2} \\
    \cos^2 x &= \frac{1 + \cos(2x)}{2}
    \end{align*}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Type 2: $\int \tan^m x \sec^n x \, dx$}

\textbf{Case 2a:} If $n$ is even
\begin{itemize}
    \item Save $\sec^2 x$ for $du$
    \item Convert rest using $\sec^2 x = 1 + \tan^2 x$
    \item Substitute $u = \tan x$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 2b:} If $m$ is odd
\begin{itemize}
    \item Save $\sec x \tan x$ for $du$
    \item Convert rest using $\tan^2 x = \sec^2 x - 1$
    \item Substitute $u = \sec x$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 2c:} Special cases
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\int \tan x \, dx = -\ln|\cos x| + C = \ln|\sec x| + C$
    \item $\int \sec x \, dx = \ln|\sec x + \tan x| + C$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \sin^3 x \cos^2 x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Strategy:} Power of $\sin x$ is odd (3), so save one $\sin x$ for $du$.

\textbf{Step 1:} Rewrite

\begin{align*}
\int \sin^3 x \cos^2 x \, dx &= \int \sin^2 x \cos^2 x \cdot \sin x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Convert $\sin^2 x$ using Pythagorean identity

\begin{align*}
&= \int (1 - \cos^2 x) \cos^2 x \cdot \sin x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Substitute $u = \cos x$, $du = -\sin x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
&= \int (1 - u^2) u^2 \cdot (-du) \\
&= -\int (u^2 - u^4) \, du \\
&= -\int u^2 \, du + \int u^4 \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Integrate

\begin{align*}
&= -\frac{u^3}{3} + \frac{u^5}{5} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5:} Back-substitute

\begin{align*}
&= -\frac{\cos^3 x}{3} + \frac{\cos^5 x}{5} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{\cos^5 x}{5} - \frac{\cos^3 x}{3} + C}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \sin^2 x \cos^2 x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Strategy:} Both powers are even, use power-reducing formulas.

\textbf{Step 1:} Apply double angle formulas

\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x &= \frac{1 - \cos(2x)}{2} \\
\cos^2 x &= \frac{1 + \cos(2x)}{2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Multiply

\begin{align*}
\int \sin^2 x \cos^2 x \, dx &= \int \frac{1 - \cos(2x)}{2} \cdot \frac{1 + \cos(2x)}{2} \, dx \\
&= \frac{1}{4}\int (1 - \cos(2x))(1 + \cos(2x)) \, dx \\
&= \frac{1}{4}\int (1 - \cos^2(2x)) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Use identity again: $\cos^2(2x) = \dfrac{1 + \cos(4x)}{2}$

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{1}{4}\int \left(1 - \frac{1 + \cos(4x)}{2}\right) dx \\
&= \frac{1}{4}\int \left(\frac{1 - \cos(4x)}{2}\right) dx \\
&= \frac{1}{8}\int (1 - \cos(4x)) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Integrate

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{1}{8}\left(x - \frac{\sin(4x)}{4}\right) + C \\
&= \frac{x}{8} - \frac{\sin(4x)}{32} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{x}{8} - \frac{\sin(4x)}{32} + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Alternative:}} Could also use $\sin x \cos x = \frac{\sin(2x)}{2}$, then $\sin^2(2x) = \frac{1-\cos(4x)}{2}$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \tan^2 x \sec^4 x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Strategy:} Power of $\sec x$ is even (4), save $\sec^2 x$ for $du$.

\textbf{Step 1:} Rewrite

\begin{align*}
\int \tan^2 x \sec^4 x \, dx &= \int \tan^2 x \sec^2 x \cdot \sec^2 x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Convert $\sec^2 x$ using identity $\sec^2 x = 1 + \tan^2 x$

\begin{align*}
&= \int \tan^2 x (1 + \tan^2 x) \cdot \sec^2 x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Substitute $u = \tan x$, $du = \sec^2 x \, dx$

\begin{align*}
&= \int u^2(1 + u^2) \, du \\
&= \int (u^2 + u^4) \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Integrate

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{u^3}{3} + \frac{u^5}{5} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5:} Back-substitute

\begin{align*}
&= \frac{\tan^3 x}{3} + \frac{\tan^5 x}{5} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{\tan^3 x}{3} + \frac{\tan^5 x}{5} + C}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \sec x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Strategy:} Multiply by $\dfrac{\sec x + \tan x}{\sec x + \tan x}$ (clever trick!)

\textbf{Step 1:} Multiply by conjugate

\begin{align*}
\int \sec x \, dx &= \int \sec x \cdot \frac{\sec x + \tan x}{\sec x + \tan x} \, dx \\
&= \int \frac{\sec^2 x + \sec x \tan x}{\sec x + \tan x} \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Substitute $u = \sec x + \tan x$

Then: $du = (\sec x \tan x + \sec^2 x) \, dx$ (exactly the numerator!)

\begin{align*}
&= \int \frac{1}{u} \, du
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Integrate

\begin{align*}
&= \ln|u| + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Back-substitute

\begin{align*}
&= \ln|\sec x + \tan x| + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\ln|\sec x + \tan x| + C}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Memorize:}} This is a standard result worth remembering!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int \sin(3x) \cos(5x) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Strategy:} Use product-to-sum formula

\textbf{Step 1:} Apply product-to-sum

\[
\sin A \cos B = \frac{1}{2}[\sin(A+B) + \sin(A-B)]
\]

With $A = 3x$ and $B = 5x$:

\begin{align*}
\sin(3x)\cos(5x) &= \frac{1}{2}[\sin(8x) + \sin(-2x)] \\
&= \frac{1}{2}[\sin(8x) - \sin(2x)]
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Integrate

\begin{align*}
\int \sin(3x)\cos(5x) \, dx &= \frac{1}{2}\int [\sin(8x) - \sin(2x)] \, dx \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\left[-\frac{\cos(8x)}{8} + \frac{\cos(2x)}{2}\right] + C \\
&= -\frac{\cos(8x)}{16} + \frac{\cos(2x)}{4} + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{-\frac{\cos(8x)}{16} + \frac{\cos(2x)}{4} + C}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.5 Summary: Trigonometric Integrals}}

\textbf{Strategy Summary:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{4cm}|p{8cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Integral Type} & \textbf{Strategy} \\
\hline
$\int \sin^m x \cos^n x \, dx$ & \textbf{Odd power:} Save one, convert rest\\
\textbf{Both even:} Power reduction \\
\hline
$\int \tan^m x \sec^n x \, dx$ & \textbf{Even $\sec$:} Save $\sec^2 x$, let $u = \tan x$\\
\textbf{Odd $\tan$:} Save $\sec x \tan x$, let $u = \sec x$ \\
\hline
Products of different args & Use product-to-sum formulas \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Essential Formulas to Memorize:}
\begin{align*}
\int \tan x \, dx &= \ln|\sec x| + C \\
\int \sec x \, dx &= \ln|\sec x + \tan x| + C \\
\int \csc x \, dx &= -\ln|\csc x + \cot x| + C
\end{align*}

\textbf{Power-Reducing Formulas:}
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x &= \frac{1 - \cos(2x)}{2} \\
\cos^2 x &= \frac{1 + \cos(2x)}{2}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting to save a factor for $du$
    \item Not recognizing when to use power reduction
    \item Sign errors with $\sin^2 x = 1 - \cos^2 x$
    \item Forgetting chain rule when integrating (e.g., $\sin(2x)$)
\end{itemize}

