Let $f$ be defined on $[a, b]$, and let $P$ be a partition with sample points $x_i^*$ in each subinterval. The \textbf{Riemann sum} is:
\[
\boxed{R_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x_i}
\]

For a regular partition with $\Delta x = \frac{b-a}{n}$:
\[
\boxed{R_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \cdot \frac{b-a}{n}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}

The Riemann sum represents the sum of areas of rectangles:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Height of $i$-th rectangle: $f(x_i^*)$
    \item Width of $i$-th rectangle: $\Delta x_i$
    \item Area of $i$-th rectangle: $f(x_i^*) \Delta x_i$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Special Cases:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Left Riemann Sum:} Use left endpoints $x_i^* = x_{i-1}$
    \item \textbf{Right Riemann Sum:} Use right endpoints $x_i^* = x_i$
    \item \textbf{Midpoint Riemann Sum:} Use midpoints $x_i^* = \frac{x_{i-1}+x_i}{2}$
\end{itemize}

