\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Approximate $\displaystyle \int_0^2 x^2 \, dx$ using a left Riemann sum with $n = 4$ rectangles.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $\Delta x$

\[
\Delta x = \frac{b-a}{n} = \frac{2-0}{4} = 0.5
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Find partition points

\begin{align*}
x_0 &= 0 \\
x_1 &= 0.5 \\
x_2 &= 1.0 \\
x_3 &= 1.5 \\
x_4 &= 2.0
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Left endpoints are sample points

\begin{align*}
x_1^* &= x_0 = 0 \\
x_2^* &= x_1 = 0.5 \\
x_3^* &= x_2 = 1.0 \\
x_4^* &= x_3 = 1.5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Compute Riemann sum

\begin{align*}
L_4 &= \sum_{i=1}^{4} f(x_i^*) \Delta x \\
&= f(0)(0.5) + f(0.5)(0.5) + f(1.0)(0.5) + f(1.5)(0.5) \\
&= (0^2)(0.5) + (0.5^2)(0.5) + (1^2)(0.5) + (1.5^2)(0.5) \\
&= 0 + 0.125 + 0.5 + 1.125 \\
&= 1.75
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{L_4 = 1.75}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Note:}} The exact value is $\int_0^2 x^2 \, dx = \frac{8}{3} \approx 2.667$, so the left sum underestimates (as expected for increasing function).

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$, then:
\[
\boxed{\lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x = \int_a^b f(x) \, dx}
\]

This limit exists and is independent of the choice of sample points $x_i^*$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Meaning:}}

As we use more and more rectangles ($n \to \infty$), the Riemann sum approaches the exact area under the curve, which we define as the definite integral.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Convergence:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Left, right, and midpoint sums all converge to the same value
    \item The integral is defined as this common limit
    \item Continuous functions are integrable (the limit exists)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.7 Summary: Riemann Sums}}

\textbf{Riemann Sum Formula:}
\[
\boxed{R_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x}
\]

For regular partition: $\Delta x = \dfrac{b-a}{n}$

\textbf{Types of Riemann Sums:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Left:} $x_i^* = x_{i-1}$ (left endpoint)
    \item \textbf{Right:} $x_i^* = x_i$ (right endpoint)
    \item \textbf{Midpoint:} $x_i^* = \frac{x_{i-1}+x_i}{2}$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Key Concept:}
\[
\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x
\]

\textbf{Approximation Quality:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Increasing function: Left underestimates, Right overestimates
    \item Decreasing function: Left overestimates, Right underestimates
    \item Midpoint sum usually gives better approximation than left or right
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Understanding:}} Riemann sums provide the conceptual foundation for definite integrals -- they formalize the idea of ``area under a curve.''

\newpage

Let $f$ be a function defined on $[a, b]$. The \textbf{definite integral} of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ is:
\[
\boxed{\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x}
\]

provided this limit exists and is the same for all choices of sample points $x_i^*$.

\textbf{Terminology:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\int$ is the \textbf{integral sign}
    \item $f(x)$ is the \textbf{integrand}
    \item $a$ is the \textbf{lower limit} of integration
    \item $b$ is the \textbf{upper limit} of integration
    \item $dx$ is the \textbf{differential}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{When the Limit Exists:}}

If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$, the limit always exists, and we say $f$ is \textbf{integrable} on $[a, b]$.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Key Difference:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& \textbf{Indefinite Integral} & \textbf{Definite Integral} \\
\hline
Notation & $\int f(x) \, dx$ & $\int_a^b f(x) \, dx$ \\
\hline
Result & Function $+ C$ & Number \\
\hline
Limits & No limits & Has $a$ and $b$ \\
\hline
$+C$ & Always included & Never included \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

