\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Given $\displaystyle \int_0^2 f(x) \, dx = 5$ and $\displaystyle \int_0^2 g(x) \, dx = -3$, evaluate:

(a) $\displaystyle \int_0^2 [2f(x) + 3g(x)] \, dx$

(b) $\displaystyle \int_2^0 f(x) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} Use linearity properties

\begin{align*}
\int_0^2 [2f(x) + 3g(x)] \, dx &= \int_0^2 2f(x) \, dx + \int_0^2 3g(x) \, dx \\
&= 2\int_0^2 f(x) \, dx + 3\int_0^2 g(x) \, dx \\
&= 2(5) + 3(-3) \\
&= 10 - 9 \\
&= 1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{1}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} Use reversal of limits

\begin{align*}
\int_2^0 f(x) \, dx &= -\int_0^2 f(x) \, dx \\
&= -5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{-5}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Given $\displaystyle \int_0^4 f(x) \, dx = 10$ and $\displaystyle \int_0^1 f(x) \, dx = 2$, find $\displaystyle \int_1^4 f(x) \, dx$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

Use interval additivity with $a = 0$, $c = 1$, $b = 4$:

\begin{align*}
\int_0^4 f(x) \, dx &= \int_0^1 f(x) \, dx + \int_1^4 f(x) \, dx \\
10 &= 2 + \int_1^4 f(x) \, dx \\
\int_1^4 f(x) \, dx &= 8
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{8}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The definite integral $\int_a^b f(x) \, dx$ represents the \textbf{net signed area} between the curve $y = f(x)$ and the $x$-axis:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Area \textbf{above} the $x$-axis: \textbf{positive} contribution
    \item Area \textbf{below} the $x$-axis: \textbf{negative} contribution
\end{itemize}

\[
\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = A_1 - A_2
\]

where $A_1$ is area above axis and $A_2$ is area below axis.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}}

The definite integral can be negative! It's not always ``area'' in the geometric sense.

