\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.8 Summary: The Definite Integral}}

\textbf{Definition:}
\[
\boxed{\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \Delta x}
\]

\textbf{Key Properties:}
\begin{align*}
\int_a^b cf(x) \, dx &= c\int_a^b f(x) \, dx \\
\int_a^b [f \pm g] \, dx &= \int_a^b f \, dx \pm \int_a^b g \, dx \\
\int_a^b f \, dx &= \int_a^c f \, dx + \int_c^b f \, dx \\
\int_a^b f \, dx &= -\int_b^a f \, dx \\
\int_a^a f \, dx &= 0
\end{align*}

\textbf{Interpretation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Definite integral = Net signed area
    \item Above $x$-axis: positive
    \item Below $x$-axis: negative
    \item Result is a \textbf{number}, not a function
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Key Differences from Indefinite Integrals:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Indefinite} & \textbf{Definite} \\
\hline
$\int f(x) \, dx$ & $\int_a^b f(x) \, dx$ \\
\hline
Function $+ C$ & Number \\
\hline
No limits & Has limits $a, b$ \\
\hline
Antiderivative & Area/Net change \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Coming Next:}} The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus will connect definite integrals to antiderivatives, giving us a powerful way to evaluate definite integrals without computing limits of Riemann sums!

\newpage

If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$, then the function $g$ defined by:
\[
g(x) = \int_a^x f(t) \, dt \quad \text{for } a \leq x \leq b
\]

is continuous on $[a, b]$, differentiable on $(a, b)$, and:
\[
\boxed{g'(x) = \frac{d}{dx}\left[\int_a^x f(t) \, dt\right] = f(x)}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

The derivative of an integral (with variable upper limit) is the original function.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Meaning:}}

If you integrate a function and then differentiate the result, you get back the original function. This shows that differentiation and integration are inverse operations!

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note the Variable:}}

Inside the integral, we use $t$ (dummy variable) instead of $x$ because $x$ is the upper limit.

