\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\int_1^x t^3 \, dt\right]$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

Using FTC Part 1 with $f(t) = t^3$:

\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int_1^x t^3 \, dt\right] = x^3
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{x^3}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} We can verify by computing the integral first:
\begin{align*}
\int_1^x t^3 \, dt &= \left[\frac{t^4}{4}\right]_1^x = \frac{x^4}{4} - \frac{1}{4} \\
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{x^4}{4} - \frac{1}{4}\right] &= x^3 \quad \checkmark
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\int_0^{x^2} \sqrt{1 + t^2} \, dt\right]$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

The upper limit is $x^2$ (not just $x$), so we need the chain rule.

Let $u = x^2$. Then:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int_0^u \sqrt{1 + t^2} \, dt\right] = \sqrt{1 + u^2} \cdot \frac{du}{dx}
\]

\textbf{Step 1:} Apply FTC Part 1
\[
\frac{d}{du}\left[\int_0^u \sqrt{1 + t^2} \, dt\right] = \sqrt{1 + u^2}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply chain rule
\[
\frac{d}{dx} = \frac{d}{du} \cdot \frac{du}{dx} = \sqrt{1 + u^2} \cdot 2x
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Substitute back $u = x^2$
\[
= \sqrt{1 + (x^2)^2} \cdot 2x = 2x\sqrt{1 + x^4}
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{2x\sqrt{1 + x^4}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{General Formula:}}
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int_a^{g(x)} f(t) \, dt\right] = f(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$ and $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$ (meaning $F' = f$), then:
\[
\boxed{\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = F(b) - F(a)}
\]

\textbf{Notation:}
\[
\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = \left[F(x)\right]_a^b = F(b) - F(a)
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

To evaluate a definite integral, find any antiderivative and compute the difference of its values at the endpoints.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Why This is Powerful:}}

Instead of computing limits of Riemann sums, we can:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find an antiderivative $F$
    \item Evaluate at endpoints: $F(b) - F(a)$
\end{enumerate}

This makes evaluating definite integrals much easier!

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}}

We don't need $+C$ when evaluating definite integrals because:
\[
[F(x) + C]_a^b = [F(b) + C] - [F(a) + C] = F(b) - F(a)
\]

The constant cancels out!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int_1^3 x^2 \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find an antiderivative

An antiderivative of $f(x) = x^2$ is $F(x) = \dfrac{x^3}{3}$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply FTC Part 2

\begin{align*}
\int_1^3 x^2 \, dx &= \left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_1^3 \\
&= \frac{3^3}{3} - \frac{1^3}{3} \\
&= \frac{27}{3} - \frac{1}{3} \\
&= 9 - \frac{1}{3} \\
&= \frac{26}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{26}{3}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Interpretation:}} The area under $y = x^2$ from $x = 1$ to $x = 3$ is $\frac{26}{3}$ square units.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int_0^\pi \sin x \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find antiderivative

An antiderivative of $\sin x$ is $-\cos x$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply FTC

\begin{align*}
\int_0^\pi \sin x \, dx &= [-\cos x]_0^\pi \\
&= -\cos(\pi) - (-\cos(0)) \\
&= -(-1) - (-1) \\
&= 1 + 1 \\
&= 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{2}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} $\sin x \geq 0$ on $[0, \pi]$, so this represents actual area (positive).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int_{-1}^2 (3x^2 - 2x + 1) \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find antiderivative

\begin{align*}
F(x) &= \int (3x^2 - 2x + 1) \, dx \\
&= x^3 - x^2 + x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply FTC

\begin{align*}
\int_{-1}^2 (3x^2 - 2x + 1) \, dx &= [x^3 - x^2 + x]_{-1}^2 \\
&= [2^3 - 2^2 + 2] - [(-1)^3 - (-1)^2 + (-1)] \\
&= [8 - 4 + 2] - [-1 - 1 - 1] \\
&= 6 - (-3) \\
&= 9
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{9}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Evaluate $\displaystyle \int_0^1 x\sqrt{1 + x^2} \, dx$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Method 1: Change limits with substitution}

Let $u = 1 + x^2$, then $du = 2x \, dx$, so $x \, dx = \frac{1}{2}du$

\textbf{Change limits:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item When $x = 0$: $u = 1 + 0^2 = 1$
    \item When $x = 1$: $u = 1 + 1^2 = 2$
\end{itemize}

\begin{align*}
\int_0^1 x\sqrt{1 + x^2} \, dx &= \int_1^2 \sqrt{u} \cdot \frac{1}{2} \, du \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\int_1^2 u^{1/2} \, du \\
&= \frac{1}{2}\left[\frac{u^{3/2}}{3/2}\right]_1^2 \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{2}{3}\left[u^{3/2}\right]_1^2 \\
&= \frac{1}{3}\left[2^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}\right] \\
&= \frac{1}{3}\left[2\sqrt{2} - 1\right] \\
&= \frac{2\sqrt{2} - 1}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Method 2: Find antiderivative first, then substitute back}

\begin{align*}
\int x\sqrt{1 + x^2} \, dx &= \frac{1}{3}(1 + x^2)^{3/2} + C
\end{align*}

Evaluate:
\begin{align*}
\left[\frac{1}{3}(1 + x^2)^{3/2}\right]_0^1 &= \frac{1}{3}(2)^{3/2} - \frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\
&= \frac{2\sqrt{2} - 1}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{2\sqrt{2} - 1}{3}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} Both methods work! Method 1 is often cleaner for definite integrals.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} A particle moves along a line with velocity $v(t) = 3t^2 - 6t$ m/s. Find the displacement from $t = 0$ to $t = 3$ seconds.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Concept:} Displacement = $\int$ velocity

\begin{align*}
\text{Displacement} &= \int_0^3 v(t) \, dt \\
&= \int_0^3 (3t^2 - 6t) \, dt \\
&= [t^3 - 3t^2]_0^3 \\
&= [3^3 - 3(3^2)] - [0 - 0] \\
&= [27 - 27] - 0 \\
&= 0
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{0 \text{ meters}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Interpretation:}} The particle returns to its starting position! The definite integral gives the \textbf{net displacement}, not total distance traveled.

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Note:}} To find total distance, we'd need to integrate $|v(t)|$ (considering when velocity is negative).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.9 Summary: Fundamental Theorem of Calculus}}

\textbf{FTC Part 1:}
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\left[\int_a^x f(t) \, dt\right] = f(x)}
\]

Differentiation and integration are inverse operations.

\textbf{FTC Part 2 (Evaluation Theorem):}
\[
\boxed{\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = F(b) - F(a)}
\]

where $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$.

\textbf{Procedure for Evaluating Definite Integrals:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find an antiderivative $F(x)$ of $f(x)$
    \item Evaluate $F(b) - F(a)$
    \item No need for $+C$ (it cancels)
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{With Substitution:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Option 1:} Change the limits when you substitute
    \item \textbf{Option 2:} Find antiderivative, substitute back, then evaluate
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Net Change Theorem:}

If $F' = f$, then:
\[
\int_a^b f(x) \, dx = F(b) - F(a)
\]

represents the net change in $F$ from $a$ to $b$.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Including $+C$ in definite integrals (unnecessary)
    \item Forgetting to change limits when substituting
    \item Computing $F(a) - F(b)$ instead of $F(b) - F(a)$
    \item Confusing FTC Part 1 and Part 2
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Exam Alert:}} FTC is fundamental! Appears in almost every calculus exam.

\newpage

If $f$ is continuous and $f(x) \geq 0$ on $[a, b]$, then the area $A$ of the region bounded by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The curve $y = f(x)$
    \item The $x$-axis
    \item The vertical lines $x = a$ and $x = b$
\end{itemize}

is given by:
\[
\boxed{A = \int_a^b f(x) \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{If $f(x) < 0$:}}

When the curve is below the $x$-axis, the integral is negative. To find the actual area (always positive), use:
\[
A = \int_a^b |f(x)| \, dx = -\int_a^b f(x) \, dx \quad \text{(when } f(x) \leq 0\text{)}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Mixed Case:}}

If $f(x)$ is sometimes positive and sometimes negative, split the integral at the zeros.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
    % Area under curve
    \draw[->] (-0.5, 0) -- (5, 0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 3) node[above] {$y$};

    % Curve
