\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the area of the region bounded by $y = x^2$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x = 1$ and $x = 3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up the integral

Since $x^2 \geq 0$ on $[1, 3]$:
\[
A = \int_1^3 x^2 \, dx
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Evaluate

\begin{align*}
A &= \left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_1^3 \\
&= \frac{27}{3} - \frac{1}{3} \\
&= \frac{26}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{A = \frac{26}{3} \text{ square units}}

\vspace{0.2cm}

If $f$ and $g$ are continuous on $[a, b]$ and $f(x) \geq g(x)$ for all $x \in [a, b]$, then the area $A$ of the region between the curves is:
\[
\boxed{A = \int_a^b [f(x) - g(x)] \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Think of it as:}}
\[
A = \int_a^b [\text{top} - \text{bottom}] \, dx
\]

\textbf{Procedure:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Sketch the curves
    \item Identify which is on top: $f(x) \geq g(x)$
    \item Find intersection points (if needed)
    \item Set up: $A = \int_a^b [f(x) - g(x)] \, dx$
    \item Evaluate the integral
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Critical:}}

Always subtract bottom from top! If you integrate $g(x) - f(x)$ when $f(x) > g(x)$, you'll get a negative area.

