A \textbf{solid of revolution} is a three-dimensional solid obtained by rotating a two-dimensional region about an axis (usually the $x$-axis or $y$-axis).

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Examples:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Rotating a rectangle about an axis $\to$ cylinder
    \item Rotating a circle about a diameter $\to$ sphere
    \item Rotating a triangle about an axis $\to$ cone
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is continuous and $f(x) \geq 0$ on $[a, b]$, then the volume of the solid obtained by rotating the region under $y = f(x)$ about the $x$-axis is:
\[
\boxed{V = \int_a^b \pi [f(x)]^2 \, dx = \int_a^b \pi y^2 \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Why This Works:}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Slice the solid perpendicular to the axis of rotation
    \item Each slice is approximately a disk (circular cross-section)
    \item Radius of disk at position $x$ is $r = f(x)$
    \item Area of disk: $A = \pi r^2 = \pi [f(x)]^2$
    \item Thickness: $dx$
    \item Volume of disk: $dV = \pi [f(x)]^2 \, dx$
    \item Sum up all disks: $V = \int_a^b \pi [f(x)]^2 \, dx$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Key Point:}}

Always square the radius: $V = \pi \int r^2 \, dx$, not $\pi \int r \, dx$!

\vspace{0.5cm}

