\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the volume of the solid obtained by rotating the region between $y = x^2$ and $y = x$ about the $x$-axis.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find intersection points

\begin{align*}
x^2 &= x \\
x^2 - x &= 0 \\
x(x-1) &= 0 \\
x &= 0 \text{ or } x = 1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Determine which is outer radius

On $(0, 1)$: $x > x^2$, so $R = x$ (outer), $r = x^2$ (inner).

\textbf{Step 3:} Set up integral

\begin{align*}
V &= \int_0^1 \pi \left(x^2 - (x^2)^2\right) dx \\
&= \pi \int_0^1 (x^2 - x^4) \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Evaluate

\begin{align*}
V &= \pi \left[\frac{x^3}{3} - \frac{x^5}{5}\right]_0^1 \\
&= \pi \left(\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{5}\right) \\
&= \pi \left(\frac{5-3}{15}\right) \\
&= \frac{2\pi}{15}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{V = \frac{2\pi}{15} \text{ cubic units}}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If the region bounded by $x = f(y)$, $x = 0$, $y = c$, and $y = d$ is rotated about the $y$-axis, the volume is:
\[
\boxed{V = \int_c^d 2\pi y \cdot f(y) \, dy}
\]

More generally, for revolution about the $y$-axis:
\[
\boxed{V = \int_a^b 2\pi x \cdot f(x) \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Shell Method Formula:}}
\[
V = \int 2\pi (\text{radius})(\text{height}) \, dx
\]

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Radius = distance from axis to shell = $x$
    \item Height = value of function = $f(x)$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Why ``Shell''?}}

Each element forms a cylindrical shell:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Circumference: $2\pi r = 2\pi x$
    \item Height: $h = f(x)$
    \item Thickness: $dx$
    \item Surface area: $2\pi x \cdot f(x)$
    \item Volume: $2\pi x \cdot f(x) \, dx$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the volume when the region bounded by $y = x^2$, $y = 0$, and $x = 2$ is rotated about the $y$-axis.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify setup

Rotating about $y$-axis, so use shell method.

\textbf{Step 2:} Identify radius and height

\begin{itemize}
    \item Radius: $r = x$ (distance from $y$-axis)
    \item Height: $h = x^2$ (value of function)
    \item Limits: $x$ goes from $0$ to $2$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3:} Set up integral

\[
V = \int_0^2 2\pi x \cdot x^2 \, dx = 2\pi \int_0^2 x^3 \, dx
\]

\textbf{Step 4:} Evaluate

\begin{align*}
V &= 2\pi \left[\frac{x^4}{4}\right]_0^2 \\
&= 2\pi \left(\frac{16}{4} - 0\right) \\
&= 2\pi (4) \\
&= 8\pi
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{V = 8\pi \text{ cubic units}}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the volume when the region between $y = x$ and $y = x^2$ is rotated about:

(a) The $x$-axis (use washer method)

(b) The $y$-axis (use shell method)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

Intersection points: $x = 0$ and $x = 1$ (from Example 46)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a) About $x$-axis (Washer Method)}

\begin{align*}
V &= \int_0^1 \pi(x^2 - (x^2)^2) \, dx \\
&= \frac{2\pi}{15} \quad \text{(from Example 46)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{V = \frac{2\pi}{15}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b) About $y$-axis (Shell Method)}

Radius: $r = x$

Height: $h = x - x^2$ (top minus bottom)

\begin{align*}
V &= \int_0^1 2\pi x(x - x^2) \, dx \\
&= 2\pi \int_0^1 (x^2 - x^3) \, dx \\
&= 2\pi \left[\frac{x^3}{3} - \frac{x^4}{4}\right]_0^1 \\
&= 2\pi \left(\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{4}\right) \\
&= 2\pi \left(\frac{1}{12}\right) \\
&= \frac{\pi}{6}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{V = \frac{\pi}{6}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} Different axes give different volumes!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.11 Summary: Volumes of Revolution}}

\textbf{Disk Method (about $x$-axis):}
\[
\boxed{V = \int_a^b \pi [f(x)]^2 \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Washer Method (hollow solid):}
\[
\boxed{V = \int_a^b \pi \left(R^2 - r^2\right) \, dx}
\]

where $R$ = outer radius, $r$ = inner radius.

\textbf{Shell Method (about $y$-axis):}
\[
\boxed{V = \int_a^b 2\pi x \cdot f(x) \, dx}
\]

Formula: $V = \int 2\pi(\text{radius})(\text{height}) \, dx$

\textbf{How to Choose:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{3cm}|p{4cm}|p{4cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Rotation Axis} & \textbf{Prefer Disk/Washer} & \textbf{Prefer Shell} \\
\hline
$x$-axis & Function of $x$ & Complex setup \\
\hline
$y$-axis & Complex setup & Function of $x$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting to square the radius in disk/washer method
    \item Confusing outer and inner radii in washer method
    \item Using wrong formula for axis of rotation
    \item Forgetting the $2\pi$ in shell method
    \item Not finding intersection points first
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Pro Tip:}} Always sketch the region and identify radii/heights before setting up the integral!

