If $f'$ is continuous on $[a, b]$, then the length $L$ of the curve $y = f(x)$ from $x = a$ to $x = b$ is:
\[
\boxed{L = \int_a^b \sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Alternative notation:}
\[
\boxed{L = \int_a^b \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2} \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Intuition:}}

For a small segment of curve:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Horizontal distance: $dx$
    \item Vertical distance: $dy = f'(x) \, dx$
    \item Arc length element: $ds = \sqrt{(dx)^2 + (dy)^2}$
\end{itemize}

\begin{align*}
ds &= \sqrt{(dx)^2 + (dy)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{(dx)^2 + [f'(x)]^2(dx)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \, dx
\end{align*}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Warning:}}

Arc length integrals are often difficult or impossible to evaluate in closed form!

