\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the length of the curve $y = \dfrac{2}{3}x^{3/2}$ from $x = 0$ to $x = 3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$

\[
\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{3}{2}x^{1/2} = x^{1/2} = \sqrt{x}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Set up arc length integral

\begin{align*}
L &= \int_0^3 \sqrt{1 + (\sqrt{x})^2} \, dx \\
&= \int_0^3 \sqrt{1 + x} \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Evaluate using substitution

Let $u = 1 + x$, $du = dx$

When $x = 0$: $u = 1$

When $x = 3$: $u = 4$

\begin{align*}
L &= \int_1^4 \sqrt{u} \, du \\
&= \int_1^4 u^{1/2} \, du \\
&= \left[\frac{u^{3/2}}{3/2}\right]_1^4 \\
&= \frac{2}{3}\left[u^{3/2}\right]_1^4 \\
&= \frac{2}{3}(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}) \\
&= \frac{2}{3}(8 - 1) \\
&= \frac{14}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{L = \frac{14}{3} \text{ units}}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Verify the arc length formula by finding the length of $y = 2x + 1$ from $x = 0$ to $x = 3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find derivative

\[
\frac{dy}{dx} = 2
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply arc length formula

\begin{align*}
L &= \int_0^3 \sqrt{1 + 2^2} \, dx \\
&= \int_0^3 \sqrt{5} \, dx \\
&= \sqrt{5} \cdot [x]_0^3 \\
&= 3\sqrt{5}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Verify using distance formula

Endpoints: $(0, 1)$ and $(3, 7)$

\begin{align*}
d &= \sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (7-1)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{9 + 36} \\
&= \sqrt{45} \\
&= 3\sqrt{5} \quad \checkmark
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{L = 3\sqrt{5} \text{ units}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Note:}} The arc length formula works correctly for straight lines!

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is continuous and $f(x) \geq 0$ on $[a, b]$, and $f'$ is continuous, then the surface area $S$ of the solid obtained by rotating the curve $y = f(x)$ about the $x$-axis is:
\[
\boxed{S = \int_a^b 2\pi f(x) \sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Alternative form:}
\[
\boxed{S = \int_a^b 2\pi y \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2} \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Formula Structure:}}
\[
S = \int 2\pi (\text{radius})(\text{arc length element}) \, dx
\]

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Radius = $y = f(x)$ (distance from axis)
    \item Arc length element = $\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \, dx$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Intuition:}}

Think of the surface as composed of thin frustums (truncated cones). The lateral surface area of each frustum is approximately $2\pi r \, ds$ where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $r = f(x)$ is the radius
    \item $ds = \sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \, dx$ is the slant height
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the surface area of a cone with base radius $r$ and height $h$ by rotating $y = \dfrac{r}{h}x$ about the $x$-axis from $x = 0$ to $x = h$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find derivative

\[
\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{r}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Set up surface area integral

\begin{align*}
S &= \int_0^h 2\pi \left(\frac{r}{h}x\right) \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{r}{h}\right)^2} \, dx \\
&= 2\pi \frac{r}{h} \sqrt{1 + \frac{r^2}{h^2}} \int_0^h x \, dx \\
&= 2\pi \frac{r}{h} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{h^2 + r^2}}{h} \int_0^h x \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Simplify and evaluate

\begin{align*}
S &= \frac{2\pi r \sqrt{h^2 + r^2}}{h^2} \left[\frac{x^2}{2}\right]_0^h \\
&= \frac{2\pi r \sqrt{h^2 + r^2}}{h^2} \cdot \frac{h^2}{2} \\
&= \pi r \sqrt{h^2 + r^2}
\end{align*}

Note that $\sqrt{h^2 + r^2} = \ell$ (slant height).

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{S = \pi r \ell}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} This matches the standard geometry formula for lateral surface area of a cone!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the surface area when $y = x^3$ from $x = 0$ to $x = 1$ is rotated about the $x$-axis.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find derivative

\[
\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Set up integral

\begin{align*}
S &= \int_0^1 2\pi x^3 \sqrt{1 + (3x^2)^2} \, dx \\
&= 2\pi \int_0^1 x^3 \sqrt{1 + 9x^4} \, dx
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Use substitution

Let $u = 1 + 9x^4$, then $du = 36x^3 \, dx$, so $x^3 \, dx = \dfrac{du}{36}$

When $x = 0$: $u = 1$

When $x = 1$: $u = 10$

\begin{align*}
S &= 2\pi \int_1^{10} \sqrt{u} \cdot \frac{du}{36} \\
&= \frac{2\pi}{36} \int_1^{10} u^{1/2} \, du \\
&= \frac{\pi}{18} \left[\frac{u^{3/2}}{3/2}\right]_1^{10} \\
&= \frac{\pi}{18} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \left[u^{3/2}\right]_1^{10} \\
&= \frac{\pi}{27} \left[10^{3/2} - 1\right] \\
&= \frac{\pi}{27} \left[10\sqrt{10} - 1\right]
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{S = \frac{\pi(10\sqrt{10} - 1)}{27} \text{ square units}}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 5.12 Summary: Arc Length and Surface Area}}

\textbf{Arc Length:}
\[
\boxed{L = \int_a^b \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2} \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Surface Area (revolution about $x$-axis):}
\[
\boxed{S = \int_a^b 2\pi y \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2} \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Key Components:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Arc length element: $ds = \sqrt{1 + (dy/dx)^2} \, dx$
    \item Surface area = $2\pi \times$ radius $\times$ arc length element
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Common Patterns:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Arc length integrals often require substitution
    \item Many cannot be evaluated in closed form (use numerical methods)
    \item Surface area combines arc length with rotation concepts
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting the square root in arc length formula
    \item Forgetting to square $f'(x)$ inside the square root
    \item Omitting $2\pi$ or the radius in surface area formula
    \item Incorrect substitution in complex integrals
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Pro Tip:}} Always verify formulas by checking simple cases (e.g., straight lines, cones).

